import { WebReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> WebReaderCopy = WebReaderCopy {
  summary: "Group a heading and related content into a section.",
  taskTitle: "Group one titled release card",
  purpose: "Keep a release title and its detail together as one card. section constructs the Html description of that section. A class is supplied, but this example includes no CSS or visual styling.",
  setup: "fn card takes String arguments named title and detail. -> html.Html<Unit> states the return type. The Unit value () carries no extra information. This example binds no event handlers. let gives h2 and p results names and types before passing their array to section’s children. The { ... } passed to section is a record of named fields, like a TS object.",
  after: [
    "children: [heading, description] produces h2 followed by p. section is the block’s final expression, so card returns its value. Calling the function twice reuses the shape with different inputs.",
    "page’s fragment puts the two cards in order without an extra enclosing tag. pub effect fn main runs println to print the string made by renderToString."
  ],
  details: [
    "In the declaration, Action is the type of value a later event may send; C is the children input type. where IntoChildren<C, Action> requires that input to be convertible to ordered children. Here Action is Unit and C is Array<Html<Unit>>.",
    "section does not create its own heading. Put an h2 or another suitable heading in children when the section needs a title. class: \"release-card\" does not by itself define the card’s appearance.",
    "The tested CLI 0.61.19 needs the shown Html<Unit>-annotated locals in this shape instead of directly nested h2 and p calls in the array. Removing them gives SES-T0201.",
    "Constructing a section is pure value construction. It neither inserts anything on screen nor checks every HTML content-model rule."
  ],
  comparisonBefore: "TS can also reuse a card function that returns a string or DOM element. It does not need a component class.",
  comparisonAfter: "Here section, h2 and p return standard Html values that are serialized afterward. The reusable unit stays an ordinary function."
}
